"""Table names for this project — the single source of truth.

Every name shows up in at least two places: the ``__tablename__`` of the
model and the string inside every ``ForeignKey`` that points at it.
Keeping them here makes both sides come from the same symbol, so
renaming a table is a one-line change and the editor's "find usages"
answers who depends on it.

Missing a foreign key while renaming does **not** fail on the spot:
SQLAlchemy resolves the target only when it configures the mappers, so
the error arrives at application startup — or inside a migration
pointing at a table that no longer exists.

This module imports nothing from the project, on purpose. It is only
strings, which is what guarantees it can never take part in an import
cycle: the models import from here, and nothing is imported back.

The user table is the one plural name here. ``USER`` is a reserved word
in standard SQL: SQLAlchemy quotes the identifier and the application
works, but a ``SELECT * FROM user`` typed by hand in ``psql`` would
return the database user instead of the table, with no error at all.
"""

USER_TABLE_NAME = "users"

__all__: list[str] = [
    "USER_TABLE_NAME",
]
